{"id":17,"course_id":8,"title":"Deployments, ReplicaSets, and Rollouts","summary":"Learn how Kubernetes keeps the desired number of application Pods running.","sequence":2,"body_simplified":"## Controller chain\nA Deployment owns ReplicaSets, and ReplicaSets own Pods. You normally change the Deployment. Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet when the Pod template changes, then gradually shifts Pods during a rollout.\n- Desired replicas means how many Pods should exist.\n- Available replicas means how many are ready enough to serve traffic.\n- Rollouts can pause, fail, or get stuck if Pods cannot become Ready.\n- Rollback uses Deployment revision history, but it cannot undo database or external state changes.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl get deploy,rs,pods -n payments -l app=checkout\n$ kubectl describe deploy checkout -n payments\n$ kubectl rollout status deploy/checkout -n payments\n$ kubectl rollout history deploy/checkout -n payments\nLook for desired/current/ready counts, old ReplicaSets still serving Pods, unavailable replicas, and rollout events.\n\n## Beginner production habit\nBefore changing replicas or images, ask what will happen to traffic and whether the application can run two versions at the same time.","body_traditional":"## Controller chain\nA Deployment owns ReplicaSets, and ReplicaSets own Pods. You normally change the Deployment. Kubernetes creates a new ReplicaSet when the Pod template changes, then gradually shifts Pods during a rollout.\n- Desired replicas means how many Pods should exist.\n- Available replicas means how many are ready enough to serve traffic.\n- Rollouts can pause, fail, or get stuck if Pods cannot become Ready.\n- Rollback uses Deployment revision history, but it cannot undo database or external state changes.\n\n## Inspection commands\n$ kubectl get deploy,rs,pods -n payments -l app=checkout\n$ kubectl describe deploy checkout -n payments\n$ kubectl rollout status deploy/checkout -n payments\n$ kubectl rollout history deploy/checkout -n payments\nLook for desired/current/ready counts, old ReplicaSets still serving Pods, unavailable replicas, and rollout events.\n\n## Beginner production habit\nBefore changing replicas or images, ask what will happen to traffic and whether the application can run two versions at the same time.","pinyin":"Scenario: A new image was released but users still hit the old version.\nChecklist:\n- Confirm the Deployment image changed.\n- Compare new and old ReplicaSet Pod counts.\n- Check whether new Pods are Ready.\n- Read rollout events before changing random fields.\nCommands:\n$ kubectl describe deploy checkout -n payments\n$ kubectl get rs -n payments -l app=checkout\n$ kubectl rollout status deploy/checkout -n payments","audio_url":null,"video_url":null,"course_slug":"platform-kubernetes-fundamentals","course_category":"Kubernetes","course_era":"Platform Academy","vocabulary":[{"id":56,"simplified":"Deployment","traditional":"Deployment","pinyin":"Controller","definition":"A controller that manages rollout history and ReplicaSets for stateless workloads."},{"id":57,"simplified":"ReplicaSet","traditional":"ReplicaSet","pinyin":"Replica controller","definition":"A controller that keeps a matching set of Pods at the requested replica count."},{"id":58,"simplified":"Rollout","traditional":"Rollout","pinyin":"Release","definition":"The process of moving from one Pod template revision to another."},{"id":59,"simplified":"Available replicas","traditional":"Available replicas","pinyin":"Readiness signal","definition":"The count of Pods that are ready and available according to Deployment rules."},{"id":60,"simplified":"Rollback","traditional":"Rollback","pinyin":"Recovery","definition":"Returning a Deployment to an earlier Pod template revision."}],"flashcards":[{"id":34,"lesson_id":17,"prompt":"What object should you normally update for a stateless app release?","answer":"The Deployment, because it owns the rollout and Pod template.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":35,"lesson_id":17,"prompt":"What does kubectl rollout status tell you?","answer":"Whether the Deployment rollout has completed, is progressing, or is stuck.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"},{"id":36,"lesson_id":17,"prompt":"Why is rollback not a complete time machine?","answer":"It changes Kubernetes objects, but external state like databases may not roll back.","pinyin":"platform","difficulty":"beginner"}]}
